Kaigi instance templates
========================

This directory is a template, not an enabled deployment. The package installs
only .sample files and this README. There is no default instance and no generated
password or JWT secret here. Package upgrades update these templates, not your
deployed instance configuration.

Choose an instance name: at most 32 lowercase letters, digits and underscores,
beginning with a letter. For example, use staff. The destination directory must
not already exist. As root, copy the example directory:

cp -R %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/example %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/staff
mv %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/staff/kaigi.conf.sample %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/staff/kaigi.conf
mv %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/staff/users.sample %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/staff/users
chmod 0600 %%PREFIX%%/etc/kaigi/staff/users

Edit kaigi.conf for the web IP/port, public URL, TLS certificate/key and media
IP/range. Every instance needs disjoint TCP/UDP allocations on overlapping bind
addresses. Edit users to add real accounts and exact meeting grants; its example
entries are comments, not usable credentials. Keep users root-owned mode 0600.
The service creates this instance's jwt-secret on first successful startup
preparation; keep it root-owned mode 0600 too. Do not copy another site's secret.

Optionally rename site-translations.json.sample, meeting-properties.json.sample
and site-assets/site.css.sample without their .sample suffixes, then customize
them. Copy your local logos into site-assets. Optional privacy examples also
need renaming/customization before use. Configure LDAP only when needed: rename
and edit ldap/ldap.conf.sample and ldap/group-meetings.json.sample, provision the
restricted search account and protected bind secret, and enable LDAP in kaigi.conf.
The service account must be able to read configuration, public assets, LDAP
settings/secrets and TLS keys; it does not need direct access to users/jwt-secret.

Enable the service and explicitly name the instance:

sysrc kaigi_enable="YES"
sysrc kaigi_instances="staff"
service kaigi start staff

For several instances use, for example, kaigi_instances="staff public" after
provisioning both directories. Directories are never automatically enabled.
Preflight validates all configured instances before start/restart. State and
recordings live under /var/db/kaigi/NAME; PID files under /var/run/kaigi/NAME;
logs under /var/log/kaigi/NAME. No files at the top level of etc/kaigi configure
an instance. See kaigi(8), kaigi.conf(5), kaigi-ldap.conf(5) and INSTANCES.md.

Service identity and LDAP secrets: the package creates the unprivileged
kaigi user and group. The service defaults to kaigi:kaigi and retains global
and per-instance daemon_user/daemon_group overrides. The root bootstrap loads
the root-owned mode-0600 LDAP bind secret once, then initializes supplementary
groups and drops to the configured group and user before starting the server
or mediasoup workers. Rotation requires restart. No secret is passed in argv,
new environment variables, launch plans or readiness files.
For interactive LDAP launches, the protected secret must be preloaded by the
service bootstrap; do not relax its permissions.

For HAProxy Unix-socket access, use WEB_SOCKET_MODE=0660 and grant the proxy
service account membership in the configured Kaigi group. Restart the proxy
to acquire that membership. Keep state directories mode 0700 and credentials
root-owned mode 0600. Before upgrading an existing deployment, stop Kaigi and
migrate ownership of each instance's writable state to the chosen service user;
do not change ownership of credentials or application code.
